<!-- FAQ SECTION -->
<section id="faq-section" class="content-section" style="background-color: #ffffff;">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
            <div class="faq-expanded">
                <aside class="faq-expanded__aside">
                    <h2 class="faq-expanded__title">Frequently Asked Questions - Section 156 Demand Notice</h2>
                    <p class="faq-expanded__lead">Get answers to common questions about income tax demand notices under Section 156, payment timelines, interest, penalties, and dispute options.</p>
                    @include('partials.bigin-form', [
                        'variant'  => 'compact',
                        'service'  => 'IT Demand Section 156',
                    ])
                </aside>
                <div class="faq-expanded__list">
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-1">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is an income tax demand notice under Section 156?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>A Section 156 notice is issued by the Assessing Officer when any tax, interest, penalty, or fine becomes payable following any order under the Income Tax Act, 1961. It specifies the exact amount due and requires payment within 30 days of service. It covers amounts arising from assessments, reassessments, TDS defaults, and rectification orders. [Section 156, IT Act 1961]</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-2">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the time limit to pay a demand notice under Section 156?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>You must pay the amount within 30 days from the date of service of the notice [Section 220(1), IT Act 1961]. In exceptional circumstances, the AO with prior approval may reduce this period. You can apply for an extension or instalment payment before expiry of 30 days with valid reasons.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-3">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What happens if I ignore an income tax demand notice under Section 156?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Ignoring has serious consequences. Interest at 1% per month accrues on the unpaid amount after 30 days under Section 220(2). The AO can levy penalty up to the demand amount under Section 221. Further, the department can initiate recovery proceedings including attachment of bank accounts, property, or salary, and in extreme cases, prosecution.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can I dispute an income tax demand notice?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. Log in to the e-filing portal and select 'Demand is Incorrect' or 'Partially Incorrect', providing reasons and uploading supporting documents. For arithmetical errors, file a rectification u/s 154. For substantive disputes, file an appeal before CIT(A) under Section 246A within 30 days of the assessment order.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the interest rate on non-payment of a Section 156 demand?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Interest under Section 220(2) of the Income Tax Act, 1961 is charged at 1% per month or part of the month on the unpaid amount after the expiry of the 30-day period. This interest continues to accrue even if an extension or instalment plan has been approved.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-6">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Section 156 notice kaise reply kare?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Log in to incometaxindia.gov.in with your PAN credentials. Go to 'Pending Actions' and select 'Response to Outstanding Tax Demand'. View the notice, verify the demand against your ITR and Form 26AS, and select the appropriate option - agree with payment, partial disagreement, or full disagreement with reasons. Complex demands mein CA ki madad lena zaroori hai.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-7">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can a Section 156 demand be stayed during an appeal?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. Under Section 220(6), you can apply to the AO to treat the demand as not due during the appeal. The AO typically requires payment of 20% of the demand. The application must be filed before the 30-day payment deadline with a copy of the appeal filing as proof.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-8">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the difference between a Section 143(1) intimation and a Section 156 demand notice?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>A Section 143(1) intimation is issued by CPC after automated processing of your ITR. If it shows a tax payable amount, it is legally deemed to be a notice of demand under Section 156 as per the proviso to Section 156. The 30-day payment obligation and default consequences apply equally.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>


            <!-- Quick Answers -->
            <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:32px;">
                <p><strong>Quick Answers</strong></p>
                <p><strong>What section governs demand notices in income tax?</strong> Section 156 of the Income Tax Act, 1961. The AO must serve the notice specifying the sum payable when any tax, interest, penalty, or fine becomes due.</p>
                <p><strong>What is the penalty for non-payment?</strong> Under Section 221(1), penalty can be up to the amount of tax in arrears. This is in addition to 1% per month interest under Section 220(2). No penalty if taxpayer proves default due to good and sufficient reasons.</p>
                <p><strong>How do I check my outstanding demand?</strong> Log in to incometaxindia.gov.in. Navigate to 'Pending Actions' and select 'Response to Outstanding Tax Demand'. All pending demands linked to your PAN will be listed.</p>
                <p><strong>Best way to respond to a Section 156 notice?</strong> Verify the demand against ITR, Form 26AS, and assessment order. Pay undisputed amounts immediately. For disputed amounts, file rectification u/s 154 for errors or appeal u/s 246A for substantive disputes, along with a stay application.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

